## The idea

Ancestors are found by **walking upward**. Build a map child → parents once. Collect **every ancestor of the first person** into a set (a stack plus a visited set, because the same ancestor can be reached two ways). Then walk upward from the second person and return true at the first ancestor in the set.

## Step by step

1. Map child → list of parents.
2. Ancestors(a): push a's parents; pop, skip if seen, add, push its parents.
3. If a has no ancestors → false.
4. Ancestors(b): if any is in a's set → true.
5. Earliest ancestor: BFS upward recording distance; keep the farthest, ties → smaller id.

## Worked example

**Input:** pairs from the report, (6, 8)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| ancestors of 6 | 3, 5 → 1, 2, 4 → 14  = {3, 5, 1, 2, 4, 14} |
| ancestors of 8 | 4 → in the set → true |
| (1, 3) | ancestors of 1 = {} → false (a parent is not a shared ancestor) |

**Result:** true for (6, 8); false for (1, 3)

## Cost

O(n) time and memory per query, n = number of pairs.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LinkedIn (reported Mar 2026, LeetCode Discuss) — common ancestor in a parent/child graph.
// An ancestor is a parent, a parent's parent, and so on (never the person themselves).
public static class Ancestry {
    // child -> its parents
    private static Dictionary<int, List<int>> ParentsOf(int[][] pairs) {
        var parents = new Dictionary<int, List<int>>();
        foreach (var p in pairs) {
            if (!parents.TryGetValue(p[1], out var list)) parents[p[1]] = list = new List<int>();
            list.Add(p[0]);
        }
        return parents;
    }

    // Every ancestor of x, walking up with an explicit stack (people can be reached by two routes: use a set).
    private static HashSet<int> AncestorsOf(Dictionary<int, List<int>> parents, int x) {
        var seen = new HashSet<int>();
        var stack = new Stack<int>();
        if (parents.TryGetValue(x, out var first)) foreach (int p in first) stack.Push(p);
        while (stack.Count > 0) {
            int y = stack.Pop();
            if (!seen.Add(y)) continue;
            if (parents.TryGetValue(y, out var up)) foreach (int p in up) stack.Push(p);
        }
        return seen;
    }

    public static bool HasCommonAncestor(int[][] pairs, int a, int b) {
        var parents = ParentsOf(pairs);
        var mine = AncestorsOf(parents, a);
        if (mine.Count == 0) return false;
        foreach (int y in AncestorsOf(parents, b)) if (mine.Contains(y)) return true;
        return false;
    }

    // Follow-up: the earliest known ancestor — the one farthest away. Ties → the smallest id. None → -1.
    public static int EarliestAncestor(int[][] pairs, int x) {
        var parents = ParentsOf(pairs);
        var dist = new Dictionary<int, int> { [x] = 0 };
        var queue = new Queue<int>();
        queue.Enqueue(x);
        int best = -1, bestDist = 0;
        while (queue.Count > 0) {                       // BFS upwards: the first distance found is the shortest
            int y = queue.Dequeue();
            if (!parents.TryGetValue(y, out var up)) continue;
            foreach (int p in up) {
                if (dist.ContainsKey(p)) continue;
                dist[p] = dist[y] + 1;
                queue.Enqueue(p);
                if (dist[p] > bestDist || (dist[p] == bestDist && p < best)) { best = p; bestDist = dist[p]; }
            }
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Is a person their own ancestor?**

No — the samples prove it: (1, 3) is false.

**Q: Two routes to one ancestor?**

A visited set — 9 is reachable from 4 and from 12.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) to build the map, O(n) for each walk.

**Q: Many queries on the same family tree?**

Precompute each person's ancestor set once (bitsets if ids are dense), then each query is a set intersection.

**Q: The family data changes while queries run.**

Build an immutable map and swap it in, rather than locking every query.

**Q: Hundreds of millions of people.**

Store parents in a key-value store; walk upward with a bounded BFS from both sides and stop at the first meet.

**Q: What did this round also cover?**

Java internals — the C# equivalents are Task vs Thread, Dictionary internals (buckets, resize) and ConcurrentDictionary (lock striping).
